MIGRATION documentation, last updated on 11 Sep 2026

A module that needs tables of its own ships the SQL that makes them.  The
build compiles that SQL into the shared object, the server checks it when
the module is loaded, and an operator applies it by hand.

Nothing about it is automatic.  That is the point.


READ THIS FIRST
===============

  A MODULE'S MIGRATIONS NEVER RUN BY THEMSELVES.

Not when the module is loaded.  Not when it is reloaded.  Not when the
server starts, not on a rehash, not on the first query.  Loading a module
tells the server what SQL exists; whether the database should run it, and
when, is an operational decision on a live network, and it stays with the
operator who is accountable for the database.

There is one exception and it is the server's own set, ircd/migrations/,
which creates the table the rest are recorded in.  That one runs at
start-up, because there is nothing to decide about it.  It is recorded
under the module name "core", which is why no module may be called that.


WHAT A MODULE SHIPS
===================

A module with migrations is a module built from a directory -- a project,
not a single .c file -- with a migrations/ subdirectory:

  modules/<type>/<name>/
    <name>.c
    migrations/
      v1_create_accounts.up.sql
      v1_create_accounts.down.sql
      v2_add_score.up.sql
      v2_add_score.down.sql

The rules, all of them checked when the module is loaded:

  * The file name is v<N>_<name>.up.sql or v<N>_<name>.down.sql, and
    nothing else.  A file in migrations/ that is not one of those is an
    error, not something to ignore -- a README in there is a mistake worth
    hearing about.

  * <name> is letters, digits and underscores.  Nothing else: it goes into
    the table and comes back out into a reply to an operator.

  * <N> starts at 1 and runs without gaps or repeats.  "v01" is not "v1";
    leading zeros are refused, because two spellings of one version is one
    version too many.

  * Every up has a down, with the same version AND the same name.

The last rule is the one people argue with, so: a migration nobody can undo
is a migration nobody can safely apply to a live network.  If the revert is
genuinely "there is nothing to undo", write a down that says so -- the
server's own v1 does exactly that, and its file explains why.

A module that breaks any of these does not load at all, and the operator
who typed /MODULE LOAD is told which file is wrong:

  -!- Could not load accounts: migrations/v2_add_score has no down file;
      every up needs its down and every down needs its up


THE .SQL FILES ARE NOT INSTALLED
================================

They are compiled into the shared object as string literals, by
cmake/GenerateMigrations.cmake, and never copied: not into the build tree,
not into the install tree, and not as module resources.

Two things follow, and both are why it is done this way:

  * A module carries its migrations wherever it goes.  A container with no
    source tree, a binary package, a module copied to another server --
    /MODULE MIGRATION APPLY works in all of them.

  * The SQL beside a module cannot be a different version from the code
    inside it, because there is no SQL beside the module.  Nobody can edit
    a migration after it has been applied and leave the server none the
    wiser.

The build embeds whatever it finds and asks no questions.  Every rule above
is enforced at load time, on purpose: a malformed migration should be an
error the operator reads, not a build failure on somebody else's machine.


THE OPERATOR COMMANDS
=====================

  /MODULE MIGRATION LIST
      Every row of the migrations table, every module at once.  This is the
      record of what has actually been applied to this database.

  /MODULE MIGRATION STATUS <module>
      What the module ships, and which of it is applied:

        Migrations for accounts:
          v1_create_accounts  applied
          v2_add_score        pending
        End of migrations for accounts (2 declared)

  /MODULE MIGRATION APPLY <module> [v<N>]
      Applies every pending migration in ascending order, stopping at the
      first failure.  With a version, applies up to and including it.

  /MODULE MIGRATION REVERT <module> [v<N>]
      Reverts ONE migration: the newest applied.  With a version, reverts
      down to and including it, newest first.

APPLY and REVERT are deliberately asymmetric.  Going forward is the
expected operation; going back destroys whatever the migration built, and
"all of them" is not something to do by leaving an argument off.

LIST and STATUS only read, and need the module_admin privilege.  APPLY and
REVERT change the schema and additionally need the CONFIG_OPERCMDS feature,
the same switch that gates LOAD and UNLOAD.

The server's own migrations are not an operator's to drive: APPLY and
REVERT refuse the name "core".  They are applied when the daemon starts.

Every applied or reverted migration is announced to the other operators and
written to the server log.  A schema change is not a private act.


THE TABLE
=========

  id                     bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY
  version                integer
  module_name            text          -- "core" for the server's own
  module_migration_name  text          -- the <name> from the file
  exec_duration          text          -- "840ms", "3.2s", "4m 11s"
  exec_by                text          -- the operator's nick
  created_at             timestamptz   -- when it ran

plus a unique index on (module_name, version), which is not decoration: it
is what stops two operators on two servers from applying the same migration
at the same moment and each believing they were first.

What is in the table is what is applied.  The server keeps no second
opinion anywhere, and reverting a migration deletes its row rather than
marking it -- a reverted migration is not applied, and the table says so.

exec_duration is text because the number of milliseconds is of no use to
anybody reading the list six months later.  It covers the script and
nothing else; the connect and the bookkeeping are the driver's overhead,
not the migration's cost.


HOW ONE RUNS
============

Each migration is one explicit transaction, on a connection opened for it
and closed after it:

    BEGIN
    <the whole .sql script>
    INSERT INTO migrations (...) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, $5) RETURNING ...
    COMMIT

A script that fails leaves neither the change nor the record, so a recorded
migration is one that actually happened.  Without that, a crash between the
two would leave the table claiming a migration that is not there, or hiding
one that is, and the next operator would apply it twice or never.

Two details of this are worth knowing, because they are exceptions to rules
the rest of the database code follows absolutely:

  * The script goes out as a string, through PQsendQuery.  It has to:
    PostgreSQL does not PREPARE DDL -- no CREATE TABLE, no ALTER TABLE, no
    CREATE INDEX -- and a migration is a script of several statements
    besides, where a prepared statement is exactly one.  This is the only
    place in the driver that sends SQL as a string, and it is safe for a
    reason that does not generalise: the string is a file the module author
    shipped, embedded at build time, taking no parameters, that nothing a
    user ever typed can reach.  The row recording it is a different matter
    -- it carries an operator's nick -- and that one is bound through
    PQexecPrepared like every other write.

  * A migration may take minutes.  It gets its own timeout, its own
    connection and a worker task of its own, so it is not held to the five
    second rule that queries are and no query queues behind it.  See
    migration_timeout below.

Steps run one at a time, and a failure stops the sequence.  The migrations
before it stay applied, which is what makes it safe to fix the broken one
and run the command again.


CONFIGURING IT
==============

    Database {
      dsn = "host=db.example.org dbname=ircu user=ircu password=secret";
      migration_timeout = 5 minutes;
    };

migration_timeout defaults to one minute and is capped at one hour.  It is
deliberately not the five second query cap: an ALTER TABLE on a table with
real data in it takes minutes, and the five second rule exists to stop a
user's command stalling the server, which is not what a migration is.
There is still a ceiling, because a migration blocked on a lock forever is
one nobody ever hears about again.

The migrations table is created when the daemon starts, as soon as there is
a database driver and a Database{} block.  A driver loaded later by hand
gets the same treatment: /MODULE LOAD postgres creates the table if it is
not there.


WRITING ONE
===========

  1. Add the pair.  Both halves, same version, same name:

       modules/commands/accounts/migrations/v3_add_email.up.sql
       modules/commands/accounts/migrations/v3_add_email.down.sql

  2. Make the up idempotent where you can -- CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS,
     ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS.  It costs nothing and it turns a half-failed
     migration into one that can simply be run again.

  3. Make the down actually undo the up.  A down that drops a column the up
     added is right; a down that drops the whole table the up altered is
     not.

  4. Build.  Nothing to register, nothing to list in a CMakeLists: the
     directory is found and embedded.

  5. Load the module, then /MODULE MIGRATION STATUS to see v3 pending, then
     /MODULE MIGRATION APPLY when the network is ready for it.

Do not edit a migration that has been applied anywhere.  Add v4.  An edited
migration is one that says one thing on the servers that ran it and another
on the servers that have not.


